        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I am not sure we are on the same page here.\r\n\r\nCursor animation, as in [`cursor` field of config](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/56509def588044fd5dcd1efe74197f1f4fe8953a/doc/mini-animate.txt#L123), does not affect cursor position in any way. It only adds visualization of its path.\r\n\r\nScroll animation, as in [`scroll` field of config](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/doc/mini-animate.txt#L135), does affect cursor position.\r\n\r\n> So basically I'm wondering if it's possible to add something to turn off vertical animations only caused by scrolloff (probably a stretch).\r\n\r\nNo, it is not possible to do that by using only 'mini.animate' functionality. And I am not sure how to detect only animations which the result of `scrolloff`.\r\n\r\n> Alternatively, if there was support for turning off certain animations runtime vs config time, I could change my configs so that way everytime I trigger lightspeed I turn off all mini.animate animations and then turn them back on afterwards.\r\n\r\nYes, this is possible. Here is an example of custom wrapper to trigger jump of 'mini.jump2d' with disabled scroll animation:\r\n```lua\r\n_G.jump = function()\r\n  MiniAnimate.config.scroll.enable = false\r\n  MiniJump2d.start()\r\n  -- Need `vim.schedule()` here to still have it disabled at animation's start\r\n  vim.schedule(function() MiniAnimate.config.scroll.enable = true end)\r\nend\r\n```\r\n\r\nDoes this solve your problem?",
